hadiths[64]{hadithnumber,text}:
"702","Narrated from Sayyiduna Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) sent Sayyiduna Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) as a judge to Yemen and said: ""O Mu'adh! Encourage the people there to acknowledge that there is no deity except Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah. If they accept this, then inform them that Allah has made five prayers obligatory upon them in every night and day. If they accept this as well, then inform them that Allah has made charity (zakat) obligatory upon their wealth, which will be taken from their rich and distributed among their poor."""
"703","Narrated from Sayyiduna Ayyub (may Allah be pleased with him): A man asked the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him), ""Tell me of an action that will admit me to Paradise."" The people said, ""What is wrong with him, why is he saying such a thing?"" The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""He is in need, and what is wrong with him? (Listen, I will tell you such an action.) Worship Allah and do not associate anything with Him, establish prayer, give zakat, and maintain the ties of kinship."""
"704","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that a Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said, ""Tell me of an action which, if I do it, I will enter Paradise."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Worship Allah and do not associate anything with Him, perform the obligatory prayers, give the obligatory zakat, and fast in Ramadan."" The Bedouin said, ""By Him in Whose hand is my soul, I will not do more than this (in worship)."" Then when he left, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever wishes to see a man from the people of Paradise, let him look at this man."""
"705","´Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that` when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away and Abu Bakr as-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) became the Caliph, some Arab tribes turned apostate. When Sayyiduna Abu Bakr as-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) intended to fight these apostates, Sayyiduna Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""How can you fight these people when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has said: 'I have been commanded to fight the people until they say La ilaha illallah (there is no god but Allah). So whoever says La ilaha illallah has protected his wealth and his life from me except by right of Islam, and his reckoning is with Allah.'"" Sayyiduna Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) replied, ""By Allah! I will surely fight the one who differentiates between prayer and zakat, because zakat is the due right upon wealth. By Allah, if they withhold from me even a young goat which they used to give in zakat during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I will surely fight them for withholding it."" Sayyiduna Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) says, ""By Allah! It was only due to this (sound judgment and firmness of resolve) that Allah had opened the chest of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) (for prudent decision), so I realized that this was the truth."""
"706","Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""On the Day of Resurrection, camels for which zakat was not paid will come in a healthy and fat state and will trample their owner under their feet. And goats for which zakat was not paid will come in a healthy and fat state, riding upon their owner, trampling him under their feet and striking him with their horns."" He (peace be upon him) said: ""One of its rights is that it should be taken to the watering place (and its milk given to the needy who are present there)."" And he said: ""None of you should come to me on the Day of Resurrection carrying a goat on his neck, the goat bleating, and then say to me, 'O Muhammad! (intercede for me),' and I will say, 'I have no authority for you in any matter. I have conveyed (the command of Allah).' Nor should anyone come to me carrying a camel on his neck, the camel grunting, and then say to me, 'O Muhammad! (intercede for me),' and I will say, 'I have no authority for you in any matter. I have conveyed the command of Allah.'"""
"707","Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever is given wealth by Allah and does not pay its zakat, then on the Day of Resurrection his wealth will be made into a bald snake resembling him, with venomous foam at the corners of its mouth. That snake will be made into a collar for his neck on the Day of Resurrection, then it will bite his jaws and say: 'I am your wealth, I am your treasure.'"" Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited this verse number ""180"" of Surah Al-Imran: ""And let not those who are miserly with what Allah has given them of His bounty think that it is good for them. Rather, it is bad for them. That with which they were miserly will be made to encircle their necks on the Day of Resurrection."""
"708","Narrated Abu Sa'id Al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him): The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Zakat is not obligatory on less than five awqiyah of gold (or fifty-two and a half tola of silver), nor on less than five camels, nor on less than five wasq of (dates)."""
"709","Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever gives charity equal to a date from his pure earnings—and Allah accepts only that which is pure—then Allah takes it in His right hand and nurtures it for the giver, just as one of you nurtures his child, until that charity becomes like a mountain."""
"710","Sayyiduna Harithah bin Wahb (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I heard the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""O people, give charity, for a time will come upon you when a man will go around with his charity but will not find anyone who will accept it. (The person) to whom he will say, 'Take it,' will say, 'If only you had brought it yesterday, I would have taken it, but today I have no need of it.'"""
"711","Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The Hour will not come until wealth becomes abundant among you and overflows. So much so that the owner of wealth will search for someone to accept his charity, and when he offers it to someone, that person will say, 'I have no need for it today.'"""
"712","Sayyiduna ‘Adi bin Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when two men came; one was complaining about his poverty and the other about the insecurity of the roads. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “As for the insecurity of the roads, it will be only a short while before (such peace will prevail that) a caravan will travel (from Madinah) to Makkah without any guard or guarantor (and no one will be able to hinder it). And as for the poor, (their situation will be such that) the Hour will not come until (there is so much wealth among people that) one of you will go around with his charity but will not find anyone to accept it. Then (remember that) surely, certainly, each one of you will stand before Allah (on the Day of Judgment). There will be no veil between him and Allah, nor any interpreter to convey his words. Then Allah, the Exalted, will say to him: ‘Did I not give you wealth?’ He will reply: ‘Yes (You did).’ Then Allah, the Exalted, will say: ‘Did I not send a Messenger to you?’ (Who informed you of the obligation of Zakat.) He will reply: ‘Yes (You did send).’ Then that person will look to his right and will see nothing but the Fire, and he will look to his left and will see nothing but the Fire. Therefore, each one of you should protect himself from the Fire, even if it is by giving half a date (in charity). And if he does not find even that, then with a good word.” (Because that too is charity.)"
"713","Abu Musa Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""A time will come upon the people when a man will go around with gold for charity but will not find anyone to accept it from him, and due to the scarcity of men and abundance of women, forty women will be seen clinging to one man."""
"714","Sayyiduna Abu Mas’ud Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) says that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would command us to give charity, one of us would go to the market and carry loads. Then if he received a mudd (of grain, etc.) as wages, he would give that as charity. (At that time, there was such poverty.) And today, some people possess one hundred thousand dirhams."
"715","Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) says: (One day) a woman came asking, and with her were her two daughters. She found nothing with me except one date, so I gave it to her. She divided the date between her two daughters and did not eat anything herself, then she got up and left. When the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us, I informed him of what had happened. The Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “Whoever is tested with any of these girls (i.e., Allah gives him daughters), then those girls will be a shield for him from the Fire.”"
"716","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that a man came to the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked, ""O Messenger of Allah! Which charity has the greatest reward?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) replied: ""That you give charity while you are healthy, desirous of wealth, fearing poverty, and hoping for affluence. And do not delay so much that when the soul reaches the throat, you say, 'Give so much to so-and-so and so much to so-and-so,' while by then, that wealth has already become the property of so-and-so (i.e., your heir)."""
"717","Narrated from Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) that some of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked him, ""Who among us will meet you first (after your passing)?"" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) replied: ""The one whose hand is the longest among you."" So they took a piece of bamboo and began to measure their hands, and Sawda (may Allah be pleased with her) had the longest hand among them. (But when Umm al-Mu'mineen Zainab bint Jahsh (may Allah be pleased with her) passed away first among them,) we realized that her hand was made long by charity (and by 'the longest hand' the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant abundance in charity, thus) she was the first among us to join the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and she loved to give charity very much."""
"718","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “(In a previous era) a man said (in his heart) that (tonight) I will give some charity. So he took his charity and went out, and (unknowingly) placed it in the hand of a thief. In the morning, people began to talk that (look, last night) charity was given to a thief. The man said: O Allah! All praise is for You alone. (Tonight again) I will give charity. So he took his charity and went out, and (unknowingly) placed it in the hand of an adulteress. In the morning, people began to talk that look, last night charity was given to an adulteress. The man said: O Allah! All praise is for You alone. My charity has reached an adulteress, well, (tonight again) I will surely give some charity. So he took his charity and went out, and (unknowingly) placed it in the hand of a rich man. In the morning, people began to talk that look, last night charity was given to a rich man. The man said: O Allah! All praise is for You alone. I have given my wealth (unknowingly) to a thief, an adulteress, and a rich man. Then a messenger (of Allah) came to him and said: (Do) not be grieved, you will receive the reward of your charity, and those to whom you gave will also benefit. Perhaps your giving charity to the thief will benefit him so that he may refrain from stealing, and the adulteress! Perhaps she may refrain from adultery, and perhaps the rich man may take heed and begin to spend from what Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has given him (in the way of Allah).”"
"719","Sayyiduna Ma'n bin Yazid (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I, my father, and my grandfather pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and it was the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) himself who arranged my engagement and performed my marriage. One day, I brought a case before him, and (the case was that) my father Yazid had set aside some gold coins for charity and had deposited them with a person in the mosque (saying, ""Give them to whomever you wish""). So I went and took those gold coins and brought them home. My father said, ""By Allah! I did not intend to give them to you."" So I brought this case before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""O Yazid! You will get the reward for the intention you made, and O Ma'n! Whatever you have taken is yours."""
"720","Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “When a woman gives charity from the food of her house, provided that her intention is not to cause harm to the household, then that woman will receive the reward for giving charity, her husband will receive the reward for earning it, and the treasurer will also receive a similar reward. And none of their rewards will be diminished (i.e., the charity is one, but all three will receive the same reward).”"
"721","Sayyiduna Hakim bin Hizam (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: ""The upper hand is better than the lower hand, and begin charity with the one who is among your family (close relatives), and the best charity is that which is given from surplus wealth. And whoever refrains from asking, Allah will keep him from asking, and whoever wants to remain self-sufficient, Allah will make him self-sufficient."""
"722","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and at that time he (peace be upon him) was on the pulpit and he (peace be upon him) was mentioning charity and avoiding asking: ""The upper hand is better than the lower hand. The upper hand is the one that gives, and the lower hand is the one that asks."""
"723","Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) says that when a petitioner would come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or something would be asked from him, he (peace be upon him) would say: ""Intercede (recommend) for him (to help him), and you will be rewarded for it, and Allah causes what He wills to be decreed through the tongue of His Prophet."""
"724","Lady Asma bint Abi Bakr Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with her) says that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: ""O Asma! Do not withhold charity, otherwise blessings from your sustenance will also be withheld."" In another hadith, these words are also mentioned: ""Do not count your wealth and property, otherwise Allah will also give to you by counting (i.e., give charity without measure, and Allah Almighty will also give you abundantly)."""
"725","Narrated from Sayyida Asma bint Abi Bakr Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) said: ""Do not withhold your wealth in your hands, otherwise Allah will withhold His mercy from you. And give charity as much as you can."""
"726","Sayyiduna Hakim bin Hizam (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I submitted, “O Messenger of Allah! Please tell me, the acts of worship that I used to do in the state of ignorance, such as giving charity, freeing slaves, and maintaining ties of kinship—will I get any reward for them?” The Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “It is because of the good deeds you had already done that you became a Muslim (meaning you will get their reward), and you became a Muslim along with all those good deeds.”"
"727","Narrated by Sayyiduna Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The Muslim treasurer who is trustworthy, carries out (the orders of) the owner (of the wealth), or his master, and gives in full the amount he is ordered to give, and does so willingly, he too will be counted among those who give charity."""
"728","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Every morning two angels descend (from the sky). One of them says: 'O Allah! Grant a substitute to everyone who spends (in Your cause).' And the other says: 'O Allah! Destroy the wealth of everyone who withholds (from spending).' """
"729","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The example of a miser and a charitable person is like two men who are wearing iron armor from their chests up to their necks. When the generous person wants to spend, his armor becomes loose or (he said) it becomes loose on his body until it covers his fingers and expands. But when the miser wants to spend something, every ring of the armor stays firmly in its place; he tries to loosen it, but it does not become loose."""
"730","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Musa Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""It is obligatory for every Muslim to give charity."" The people asked, ""O Messenger of Allah! What if someone is not able to do so?"" He (peace be upon him) said: ""He should work with his hands, benefit himself, and give charity."" The people asked, ""What if he is not able to do that either?"" He (peace be upon him) said: ""He should help a needy and oppressed person."" The people asked, ""What if he is not able to do that either?"" He (peace be upon him) said: ""Then he should do good and refrain from evil; this is charity for him."""
"731","Narrated from Umm Atiyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) that Naseehah Ansariyyah (may Allah be pleased with her), whose real name is actually Umm Atiyyah, was sent a goat as charity. She gave some of it to Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her). Then when the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) came home, he said: ""Do you have anything?"" Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) replied: ""Nothing except what is from the goat (meat) given as charity, which Naseebah sent."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Bring it, for it has reached its rightful place (meaning, now for us it is not charity)."""
"732","Narrated from Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that Abu Bakr Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote for them what Allah had commanded His Messenger: that whoever is liable for a one-year-old she-camel in charity and does not have it, but has a two-year-old she-camel, then it will be accepted from him, and the collector of charity will return twenty dirhams or two goats to him. And if he does not have a one-year-old she-camel (nor a two-year-old one, rather) but has a three-year-old camel, then it will be accepted from him and nothing will be given to him along with it."
"733","`Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) says that` Abu Bakr Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote down for them what Allah’s Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had prescribed (regarding zakat), (and in it was this instruction:) “Wealth should not be combined out of fear of charity, nor should combined wealth be separated.”"
"734","Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that Abu Bakr as-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) had written down for their guidance those matters which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had made obligatory regarding Zakat (and it included this statement): That any wealth jointly owned by two partners, after paying Zakat, they should consider it equally divided between themselves."
"735","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Sa'id Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) that a Bedouin asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about migration. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Woe to you, the matter of migration is very difficult. Do you have some camels from which you give zakat?"" He replied, ""Yes."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""(Then you do not need to migrate) Worship even across the rivers (i.e., in your own land), for Allah will not let any of your worship go to waste."""
"736","´Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that´ Sayyiduna Abu Bakr Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote for them the obligations of charity (zakat) which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had commanded (and in it was this content): That for the person who, in zakat of camels (for 61 to 75), is required to give a four-year-old she-camel, and he does not have it, but he has a three-year-old she-camel, then that should be taken from him, and along with it, if he can provide two goats or twenty dirhams, that should be taken from him. And for the person who, in zakat (for 46 to 60 camels), is required to give a three-year-old she-camel, and he does not have it, but he has a four-year-old she-camel, then that should be taken from him, and the zakat collector should give him twenty dirhams or two goats. And for the person who is required to give a three-year-old she-camel in zakat, and he does not have it, but he has a two-year-old she-camel, then that should be taken from him, and he should also give twenty dirhams or two goats along with it. And for the person who, in zakat (for 36 to 45 camels), is required to give a two-year-old she-camel, and he has a three-year-old she-camel, then that should be accepted from him, and he should be given back two goats or twenty dirhams. And for the person who is required to give a two-year-old she-camel in zakat, and he does not have it, but he has a one-year-old she-camel, then that should be taken from him, and he (the zakat giver) should also give twenty dirhams or two goats along with it."
"737","‘Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) says that when Sayyiduna Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) sent him to Bahrain to collect Zakat, he wrote him this document: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. These are the obligatory charities, i.e., the Zakat duties, which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made obligatory upon the Muslims, and about which Allah Almighty commanded His Messenger. So, whoever among the Muslims is asked for Zakat according to this document should pay it, and whoever is asked for more than what is in this document should not pay it. For twenty-four or fewer camels, one goat (there is no Zakat on fewer than five). Then, when there are twenty-five to thirty-five camels, one-year-old she-camel; then, when there are thirty-six to forty-five camels, one two-year-old she-camel; then, when there are forty-six to sixty camels, one three-year-old she-camel fit for mating must be given; then, when there are sixty-one to seventy-five camels, one four-year-old she-camel; then, when there are seventy-six to ninety camels, two-year-old she-camels; then, when there are ninety-one to one hundred and twenty camels, two three-year-old she-camels fit for mating must be given. Then, when there are more than one hundred and twenty, for every forty, one two-year-old she-camel, and for every fifty, one three-year-old she-camel must be given. And whoever has only four camels, there is no Zakat on them, but if the owner wishes, he may give. Then, when there are five, one goat must be given as Zakat. And for goats that graze in the wild, when they are forty to one hundred and twenty, one goat is obligatory; then, when they are more than one hundred and twenty up to two hundred, two goats; then, when they are more than two hundred up to three hundred, three goats; then, when they are more than three hundred, for every hundred, one goat. And if someone has fewer than forty goats that graze in the wild, even if only one less, there is no Zakat on them, but if the owner wishes, he may give. And Zakat is only obligatory on those camels, cows, and goats that graze in the wild for more than six months. If they are fed fodder, etc., from the owner for more than six months, then there is no Zakat on them. And apart from these three animals, Zakat is not obligatory on any other animal, except that buffalo is a type of cow. And for silver, the fortieth part is Zakat, provided it reaches two hundred dirhams. And if it is one hundred and ninety dirhams, there is no Zakat on it, but if the owner wishes, he may give.’"
"738","Narrated Anas (may Allah be pleased with him): Abu Bakr As-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote a document for him (containing the rulings of Zakat) which Allah had commanded His Messenger (peace and blessings be upon him). (It also contained this statement:) ""In Zakat, an old, defective, or male animal should not be taken, i.e., a goat, etc. Unless the one giving charity wishes so."" (Then there is no harm in taking a male animal.)"
"739","Narrated from Sayyiduna Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Sayyiduna Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) to Yemen (this hadith has already been mentioned, see the Book: On Zakat ... Chapter: The obligation of Zakat is established by Shariah ...), in this narration it is mentioned that he (peace be upon him) said to him: ""You are going to the People of the Book (that is, the Jews and Christians)...."" and then (he) mentioned the rest of the hadith. Then at the end (the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to Sayyiduna Mu'adh, may Allah be pleased with him): ""...and avoid taking the best of people's wealth."""
"740","Narrated Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him): Among all the Ansar in Madinah, Abu Talha (may Allah be pleased with him) possessed the most wealth, especially in gardens, and the most beloved of his gardens to him was called Birha’, which was situated opposite the Prophet’s Mosque. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to go there and drink from its pleasant water. Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: When the verse (Surah Al-Imran, verse 29) was revealed: “You will never attain righteousness until you spend from that which you love,” Abu Talha (may Allah be pleased with him) stood before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah! Allah, the Most Great and Exalted, says: “You will never attain righteousness until you spend from that which you love.” Indeed, the most beloved of my wealth to me is Birha’, and I give it as charity for Allah. I hope for its reward with Allah, so spend it wherever you see fit, O Messenger of Allah. Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Messenger of Allah said: “Well done! That is a profitable wealth, that is a profitable wealth. I have heard what you said, and I think you should distribute it among your relatives.” So Abu Talha (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Messenger of Allah! I will do so. Thus, he distributed it among his relatives and the sons of his uncle."
"741","´Sayyiduna Abu Sa'id Khudri رضي الله عنه narrates that` the Messenger of Allah صلى الله عليه وسلم went to the Eidgah on Eid al-Adha or Eid al-Fitr.... This hadith has already passed (see the Book: Description of the Two Eids... Chapter: Going to the Eidgah without a pulpit...). In this narration, it is mentioned that when the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم returned and went towards his house, Sayyidah Zainab, the wife of Sayyiduna Ibn Mas'ud, came and began to seek permission to present herself before the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم. It was said, ""O Messenger of Allah! Zainab (has come)."" The Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم asked, ""Which Zainab?"" (because there were many women named Zainab). So it was said, ""The wife of Sayyiduna Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنهما."" The Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم said, ""Alright, give her permission."" She was given permission (when she came), she said, ""O Messenger of Allah! Today you صلى الله عليه وسلم have ordered us (women) to give charity, and I have some jewelry. I wished to give it in charity, but Ibn Mas'ud said that he and his children are most deserving that I give it to them as charity."" So the Messenger of Allah صلى الله عليه وسلم said, ""Ibn Mas'ud has spoken the truth. Your husband and your children are most deserving that you give charity to them."""
"742","Narrated by Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he (peace be upon him) said: ""Zakat is not obligatory on a Muslim for his (servant) slave and his (riding) horse."""
"743","`Sayyiduna Abu Sa'id Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that` one day the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting on the pulpit and we were sitting around him. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Among the things I fear for you after me is that the freshness and adornment of the world will be opened up for you."" So a man said, ""O Messenger of Allah! (What harm can come from abundance of wealth? Wealth is such a good thing.) Can a good thing also produce evil?"" (Upon hearing this) the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained silent. Then it was said to him, ""What is the matter with you that you are conversing with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he is not speaking to you?"" Then we thought that revelation was descending upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (that is why he remained silent). So when the revelation was completed, it is said that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped the sweat from his face and said: ""Where is the questioner?"" As if the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) liked his (question). Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""(Yes, it is true that) a good thing does not produce evil, but (evil arises from its misuse). Look, along with the spring season, such grass also grows that (when eaten by grazing animals) kills or makes them sick. But among those who graze on this greenery, the one who eats until both his sides are filled, then stands in the sun, then defecates and urinates, and (after that) grazes again (does not eat excessively all at once, he does not die). And indeed, this wealth is a pleasant green pasture. So what a good wealth it is for a Muslim who gives from it to the poor, the orphan, and the traveler."" Or the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said something similar: ""And indeed, whoever takes this wealth unlawfully will be like the one who eats but is never satisfied, and that wealth will testify against him on the Day of Resurrection."""
"744","The hadith of Lady Zainab, the wife of Abdullah bin Mas’ud (may Allah be pleased with her), has just been mentioned (see the chapter: Spending Zakat on one’s relatives...). In this narration, she also says that I went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and found an Ansari woman at the door who had come with a need similar to mine. Then, Sayyiduna Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) passed by us, so we said to him: Ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on our behalf whether it is sufficient for me to spend (my wealth) on my husband and the orphans under my care? And we told (Sayyiduna Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him) not to mention our names. (But when Sayyiduna Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, went and asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: “Who are these two women?” Sayyiduna Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) said, “Zainab.” The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked, “Which Zainab?” He replied, “The wife of Abdullah bin Mas’ud (may Allah be pleased with them both).” The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “Yes (it is sufficient, rather) she will have a double reward: the reward of maintaining kinship and the reward of giving charity.”"
"745","Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: I asked, ""O Messenger of Allah! Will I have any reward if I spend (my wealth) on the children of Abu Salamah, for they are my sons?"" He (peace be upon him) replied: ""Spend on them; whatever you spend on them, you will be rewarded for it."""
"746","Narrated Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed a man to collect charity, i.e., zakat. That man went and brought the collected charity, then said: Ibn Jameel, Khalid bin Al-Walid, and Abbas bin Abdul Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with them) did not give (zakat). So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Ibn Jameel refused because he was poor, and Allah and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made him rich (indeed, his refusal indicates rebellion). As for Khalid bin Al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him), you are wronging Khalid (may Allah be pleased with him) (why did you ask him for zakat? Because) he has reserved his armors and weapons for the cause of Allah. As for Abbas bin Abdul Muttalib, he is the uncle of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); zakat is obligatory upon him, and the same amount again (so I will pay it)."""
"747","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Sa'id Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) that some people from among the Ansar asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for something, so he gave them. Then they asked him again, and he gave them again, until whatever wealth he had was finished. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When I have wealth, I will not withhold it from you. But (remember that) whoever refrains from asking, Allah will protect him (from poverty and need), and whoever shows self-sufficiency, Allah will make him self-sufficient, and whoever is patient, Allah will make him patient. And (listen) no one has been given a blessing better and more extensive than patience (so do not let go of patience)."""
"748","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, it is better for one of you to take a rope, gather firewood, carry it on his back, and sell it, than to go to someone and ask him (for something), whether he gives him or refuses."""
"749","Narrated by Sayyiduna Zubair bin Al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: ""Indeed, for one of you to take his rope, gather a bundle of wood on his back, bring it and sell it, and by this means Allah preserves his honor, is better for him than asking people (even though it is not known) whether they will give him or not."""
"750","Sayyiduna Hakim bin Hizam (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I once asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for something, and he gave it to me. Then I asked again, and he gave it to me. I asked again, and he gave it to me. After that, he said: ""O Hakim! This wealth is a sweet and lush thing. Whoever takes it without greed, there is blessing in it for him, and whoever takes it with greed, there is no blessing in it for him, and he is like the one who eats but is never satisfied. And the upper hand is better than the lower hand."" Sayyiduna Hakim (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah! By the One Who has sent you with the truth, I will not take anything from anyone after you until I depart from this world."" So, when Sayyiduna Abu Bakr as-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) became the Caliph, he kept calling Hakim (may Allah be pleased with him) to give him his stipend (which was fixed for all the companions), but he refused to accept it. Then Sayyiduna Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), during his caliphate, called him to give him his stipend, but he refused to accept it. So Sayyiduna Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O Muslims! I make you witness that I am offering Hakim (may Allah be pleased with him) his right, but he refuses to take his share from this booty."" In short, Sayyiduna Hakim (may Allah be pleased with him) did not take anything from anyone after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he passed away."
"751","Ameer-ul-Mu'mineen Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) says that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to give me wealth, so I would say: ""Give it to the one who is more in need than me."" So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Take it. When some wealth comes to you from this (source) and you are not greedy for it, nor have you asked for it, then accept it. And whatever you do not get, do not worry about obtaining it, and do not pursue it."""
"752","Narrated Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever habitually begs from people will come on the Day of Resurrection in such a state that there will not be a piece of flesh on his face."" And the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""On the Day of Resurrection, the sun will be brought near until the sweat reaches up to half the ear, then all people will be in that state when suddenly they will seek help from Adam (peace be upon him), then from Musa (peace be upon him), then from Muhammad (peace be upon him)."""
"753","Narrated by Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: ""A poor person is not the one who goes around to people and gets a morsel or two, or a date or two (i.e., who fills his stomach by begging morsel by morsel from people), but the poor person is the one who does not have enough to suffice him, yet he is modest and does not beg persistently from people."""
"754","Sayyiduna Abu Humayd Sa’idi (may Allah be pleased with him) says: We fought in the Battle of Tabuk along with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When he (peace be upon him) reached (the place) Wadi al-Qura, (he saw that) a woman was in her garden. The Noble Prophet (peace be upon him) said to his companions: “Estimate (how many dates are in her garden).” And the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) estimated it to be ten wasqs. Then he (peace be upon him) said to her: “Take care to see how many dates are produced from it.” Then when we reached (the place) Tabuk, he (peace be upon him) said: “Tonight there will be a strong wind, so no one should stand up, and whoever has a camel with him should tie it.” So we tied the camels, and a strong wind blew. A man (by chance) stood up, and the wind threw him onto the mountain called Tayy. And (in this battle) the king of Aylah sent a white mule to the Noble Prophet (peace be upon him) and also a sheet for him to wear. The Prophet (peace be upon him) kept him as the ruler of that place. Then when (after the end of the battle) he returned and reached Wadi al-Qura, the Prophet (peace be upon him) asked that woman: “How many dates were produced in your garden?” She replied: “Ten wasqs, just as you had estimated.” The Noble Prophet (peace be upon him) said: “I wish to reach Madinah quickly, so whoever among you can hasten to go with me, let him do so.” When Madinah came into view for the Prophet (peace be upon him), he said: “This is Taba (another name for Madinah) coming.” Then when he (peace be upon him) saw Uhud, he said: “This is the mountain that loves us and we love it. Shall I not tell you about the best houses among the houses of the Ansar?” The companions said: “Yes.” He (peace be upon him) said: “The houses of Banu Najjar, then the houses of Banu ‘Abd al-Ashhal, then the houses of Sa’idah, or (he said) the houses of Banu Harith ibn Khazraj. (These houses are the best, otherwise) there is goodness in all the houses of the Ansar.”"
"755","Narrated by Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: ""On that which is irrigated by the water of the sky, i.e., rain, or by springs, or grows by itself, one-tenth (ushr) is obligatory, and on that which is irrigated by well water, half of one-tenth (i.e., one-twentieth) is obligatory."""
"756","Narrated Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him): Dates would start coming to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) as soon as they were harvested. Sometimes this person would come bringing his dates, sometimes that person would come bringing his dates, until heaps of dates would be piled up in front of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). (One day) Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) and Husain (may Allah be pleased with him) began to play with those dates. One of them took a date and put it in his mouth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) saw this and immediately took the date out of his mouth and said: ""Do you not know that the family of Muhammad does not eat charity?"""
"757","Amir al-Mu’minin Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) says that (once) I gave a horse in the way of Allah for riding, but the person who had the horse did not value it. I intended to buy it back (because) I thought he would sell it cheaply. Therefore, I asked the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) about this, and he said: “Do not buy it, even if he gives it to you for only one dirham. Indeed, the one who takes back his charity is like the one who eats his own vomit.”"
"758","Narrated Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a dead goat which had been given in charity to a slave woman of Umm al-Mu'mineen Maymunah (may Allah be pleased with her). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Why do you not benefit from its skin?"" The people said: ""It was dead."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Its prohibition is only in eating it."""
"759","Narrated from Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that some meat was brought before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which had been given to Barirah (may Allah be pleased with her) as charity. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""(This meat) was charity for Barirah (may Allah be pleased with her) and is a gift for us."""
"760","The hadith of Sayyiduna Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) and the mention of sending him to Yemen has already been mentioned (see the chapter: The obligation of Zakat is established from the Shariah...). Here, in this narration, it is stated that (the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said): ""(O Mu'adh!) Also inform them that Allah Almighty has made Zakat obligatory upon them, which will be taken from their wealthy and distributed among their poor. If they accept this from you, then do not take the best of their wealth (as Zakat), and beware of the supplication of the oppressed, for there is no barrier between it and Allah Almighty."""
"761","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Awfi (may Allah be pleased with him) that it was the noble habit of the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) that whenever someone brought his charity to him, he (peace be upon him) would say: ""O Allah! Have mercy on the children of so-and-so."" So once, my father brought his charity to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and he (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""O Allah! Have mercy on the children of Abu Awfi."""
"762","`Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him):` ""A man from the Children of Israel asked another for a loan of one thousand dinars, so he gave them to him. (Coincidentally) the debtor went on a journey, and the time for repayment arrived (but there was a sea between them). So he went to the sea, but found no means (to ride and reach the creditor to repay the debt). Therefore, he took a piece of wood, made a hole in it, placed one thousand dinars inside, and threw it into the sea, and prayed to Allah to deliver it to the creditor. So the man who had given the loan (coincidentally, one day passed by the sea) saw that piece of wood. He picked it up for his family’s firewood (then the entire hadith was narrated, at the end of which was this): When he split the wood, he found his money inside it."""
"763","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""There is no compensation for injury caused by an animal, nor for one who falls into a well, nor for one who dies while working in a mine. However, there is a fifth (share) on treasure found (in the ground)."""
"764","`Sayyiduna Abu Humayd Sa'idi رضي الله عنه narrates that` the Messenger of Allah صلى الله عليه وسلم appointed a man to collect the charity from the (tribe of) Banu Sulaym. This man was called Ibn Lutbiyyah. Then when he (collected the charity and) returned, the Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم took an account from him."
"765","Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) says that one day in the morning I took the newborn son of Abu Talha (may Allah be pleased with him), Abdullah, to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) so that he (peace and blessings be upon him) could perform tahnik for him, that is, chew a date and put it in his mouth. I found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) in such a state that he (peace and blessings be upon him) had a branding iron in his hand and he (peace and blessings be upon him) was branding the camels given in charity with it."
